f Equations Using Gaussian Elimination" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049422 #edb/ref 1033 151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049422 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "f3a09ebe-2d8e-5a69-8681-eb398a98c3dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050825 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050825 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050825 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050825 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050825 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050825 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050825 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186100866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050825 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186100867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050825 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186100868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050825 #edb/ref 1031 "Multiplying a Matrix by a Column Vector" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050825 #edb/ref 1033 1195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050825 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "69b57f07-ad86-5963-8f81-f63cb01f239f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119623 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4810" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119623 #edb/ref 1071 "The matrix $A$ and vectors $\\mathbf{b}_1$, $\\mathbf{b}_2$, $\\mathbf{b}_3$ are given below. Which of the vectors lie in $\\textrm{Null}(A)?$\n\n![](images/53/53e17b54ff077d45401bdc4754ccdcff5d0cad20e8d5b477b7a9e0aec37ab52c.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119623 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fe2f3934-fa5c-5a59-be7e-00b6e4889063" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119623 #edb/ref 1074 "A vector $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ if $A\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we need to compute the product of $A$ and each of the given vectors and check whether it is equal to $\\mathbf{0}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{b}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -5 & 10 \\\\ -8 & 16\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-27 \\\\ 45 \\\\ 72\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ A{b}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -5 & 10 \\\\ -8 & 16\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\\,✓ \\\\ A{b}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -5 & 10 \\\\ -8 & 16\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-15 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 40\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $\\mathbf{b}_2$ lies in $\\textrm{Null}(A).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119624 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4812" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119624 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix ![](images/c8/c85268d7db6efb171da25432988c0ee99252f4cdb10f8a1cc24f31af5b2e239d.svg) and the vector ![](images/c7/c7095b22e7a8cbbeefc6b75b63886550a4c7c35f23585e2c0006ddf14b424c28.svg) Find the value of $k$ such that $\\mathbf{b}\\in\\textrm{Null}(A).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119624 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e38db5fa-1753-5176-bb5e-a077ea63ddec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119624 #edb/ref 1074 "If $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Null}(A),$ then we must have $A\\mathbf{b} = \\mathbf{0}.$ Substituting the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ab & =0 \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -6 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ k \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6+2k-12 \\\\ -6+3k-3 \\\\ 12-6k+6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2k-6=0 \\\\ 3k-9=0 \\\\ -6k+18=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for each of these equations, we obtain $k=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119625 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4814" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119625 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix ![](images/17/17cd2f2b0846461e1ed6480f82ce576fc82e5066258d80ed6f7084b0d55ca4a9.svg) Find $\\textrm{Null}(A).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119625 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1e59cd29-ea54-5b90-8215-60c81d1b6dbb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119625 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.$ First, we find the row echelon form of $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -12 \\\\ 3 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second equation tells us that $x_2=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-3(0) & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that ![](images/db/db533ede6312d49204e619379dfff639c0871f37ee997ce152ec35bd64105926.svg) is the only solution to this system.\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\left\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119812 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119812 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119812 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119812 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119812 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119812 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119812 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119812 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119812 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186417739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119812 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Whether a Vector Lies in the Null Space of a Given Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119812 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119623